<div class="section-eyebrow">The framing problem</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Almost everything we hear about Hormuz is about oil.</h2>

<p class="prose">Six months into the closure, the oil numbers are well covered: transit down to a few percentage points of normal, war risk premiums at multiples of the pre-crisis rate, Brent up more than twenty percent. But the Middle East Gulf is also a major global source of urea and sulphur which are both byproducts of the region's oil and gas refining, and that trade has been disrupted alongside oil since the closure began on 28 February. India, Sri Lanka, and Thailand are the most exposed importers. This also has knock-on food security implications: urea is fertilizer, and a fertilzer supply shock reaches a farmer's input costs the next planting season.</p>

<p class="prose">This piece was put together for a live segment on the Granular podcast with Holly Birkett, recorded 27 August 2026.</p>

<div class="section-eyebrow">The correction</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Oman provides supplies a great deal of fertilizer and it is outside the Hormuz Strait. But...</h2>

<p class="prose">The easiest way to state Gulf fertilizer exposure is in a single country-level number: "India's urea imports are roughly 85% dependent on the Gulf." But a large part of India's urea supply comes from Oman's main fertilizer export terminal, Sur which sits on the Gulf of Oman coast, seaward of the strait. A vessel loading at Sur never enters the Middle East Gulf and is not as dependent on Hormuz being open as it would seem.</p>

<p class="prose">India's OMIFCO joint venture with Oman provides a dedicated bilateral supply arrangement between the two countries and means that Oman supplies about 57.8% of India's Gulf-sourced urea by value. India's still has a Hormuz-specific urea dependency closer to 36%. Sri Lanka and Thailand have smaller Oman shares, 15.3% and 20.9% respectively, which means that Sri Lanka is roughly 66% dependent on Hormuz and Thailand roughly 53%.</p>

<div class="section-eyebrow">The market and the physical geography disagree</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Oman is outside the strait but it is not outside the war risk zone.</h2>

<p class="prose">The physical correction above does not mean Oman-sourced cargo is priced as safe. The Joint War Committee's Listed Areas circular, JWLA-033, issued 3 March 2026, explicitly names Oman as a listed country, and by the circular's  definition, a listed country's ports and coastal waters out to 12 nautical miles are covered unless specifically varied. Sur, Sohar, Duqm, Salalah, and Muscat are all inside the current war risk boundary, which is drawn around the whole theatre, the Middle East Gulf, Gulf of Oman, Arabian Sea, Gulf of Aden and Southern Red Sea, on regional threat exposure, not on which vessels physically transit a specific strait.</p>

<p class="prose">Physically, Omani urea does not need Hormuz to reopen, but commercially, the market has not priced Oman as a safe alternative, because the war risk boundary was drawn on the region's overall threat exposure, missile and drone range, general Gulf of Oman risk, rather than on strait transit specifically. A cargo book that assumes Oman-sourced fertilizer is both physically and commercially insulated from the Hormuz crisis would be wrong on the second count even where it is right on the first.</p>

